<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Add Physics Objects in Blender for Godot"
  description="Configure Blender objects for Godot physics with FPE mass, friction, bounciness, collision, and continuous collision detection settings."
  summary="Physics is where a pretty prop starts feeling like part of the world. A mug should settle, a rubber ball should bounce, and a heavy crate should resist being shoved. Author those material expectations beside the object itself."
  relatedSlugs={[
    "holdable-items-godot",
    "build-levels-in-blender-for-godot",
    "interactive-objects-godot",
  ]}
  fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot"
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>You will make:</strong> a Blender prop that imports as a physical Godot
    object with intentional weight, grip, bounce, and collision behavior.
  </div>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/physics-object-checkbox.png"
    alt="Teacup-rigid selected in Blender with the FPE Physics checkbox enabled"
    caption="Keep Godot's supported -rigid name suffix and enable Physics in FPE when you also need authored body settings."
  />
  <h2>First, make it a physics body</h2><p>
    The FPE <strong>Physics</strong> checkbox does not turn an ordinary mesh into
    a rigid body. Godot's standard glTF importer decides the node and collider type
    from the Blender object name.
  </p><ol>
    <li>
      Work at real-world scale, apply scale, and set the origin where rotation
      and balance make sense.
    </li><li>
      For a movable prop, add <code>-rigid</code> to the Blender object name—for
      example, <code>Teacup-rigid</code>. Godot imports it as a <code
        >RigidBody3D</code
      > with collision.
    </li><li>
      For immovable level geometry, use <code>-col</code> for a visible mesh with
      static collision. Godot also supports <code>-convcol</code>, <code
        >-colonly</code
      >, and <code>-convcolonly</code> for other collider needs.
    </li><li>
      Only after choosing the body type, open Blender's <strong
        >Item → Folded Paper Engine</strong
      > panel and check <strong>Physics</strong>. That exposes the FPE material
      and motion settings applied to the imported physics body.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/physics-settings-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Physics panel showing mass friction bounce and continuous collision detection"
    caption="Tune physical behavior in Blender: this light teacup uses modest friction, very little bounce, and continuous collision detection."
  />
  <h2>Describe how the prop should feel</h2><ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Mass:</strong> controls inertia. Start with a plausible value and compare
      related props rather than chasing perfect simulation.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Friction:</strong> increase it for grippy crates and reduce it for
      slippery objects.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Bounciness:</strong> keep ordinary furniture low; raise it deliberately
      for balls or playful props.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Continuous Collision Detection:</strong> enable it for small or fast
      objects that could otherwise pass through thin colliders.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Export a tiny physics test</h2><ol>
    <li>Place the prop above a collidable floor.</li><li>
      Export with <strong>Custom Properties</strong> enabled.
    </li><li>Load the GLB through FPE and let the object fall.</li><li>
      Check resting orientation, sliding, bounce, tunneling, and scale.
    </li><li>
      Change one property at a time in Blender and re-export until the result
      communicates the material you imagined.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Common problems</h2><ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Explodes or spins wildly:</strong> inspect overlapping collision, origin
      placement, scale, and spawn position.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Falls through the floor:</strong> verify both objects have collision;
      use continuous detection only when speed or size justifies it.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Everything feels weightless:</strong> check scale first, then compare
      mass and damping in Godot.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Imported edits disappear:</strong> fix authored physics in Blender
      or a stable extension, not inside the generated scene.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      Godot's standard name suffix creates the physics body and collision. FPE
      then applies the Blender-authored mass and continuous collision detection
      to rigid bodies, plus friction and bounciness to supported physics bodies.
      You can combine a <code>-rigid</code> prop with <strong>Holdable</strong> for
      first-person carrying.
    </p><p>
      <a
        href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.5/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_3d_scenes/node_type_customization.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Read Godot's official import-suffix reference</a
      > and the <a href="/docs/fpe-physics-and-colliders.html"
        >FPE physics and collider reference</a
      >.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
